INT. LAUNDROMAT, LATE NIGHT

Twelve dryers, three working. A TV on the wall plays a muted weather channel.

NADIRA FAROUK (30s, scrubs, hair in a clip) folds towels at a long table, sorts socks into pairs, pulls a stray sock from her pocket and drops it into a lost-and-found bucket, all without looking up. Across the room, her brother-in-law CASIMIR ODUYA (40s, security jacket) feeds quarters into a dryer, kicks the door shut, and slumps onto a plastic chair. In the corner, a young woman, LINNEA HARTWELL-SATO (20s, headphones, textbook), highlights a page and glances at the clock every half minute.

An intercom crackles on the wall.

INTERCOM (V.O.)
(tinny, bored)
Attention customers. The attendant is on break. Please do not feed the machines your feelings.

CASIMIR
That's new.

NADIRA
It's the owner. He thinks he's funny.

CASIMIR
He is not.

A small pause. A dryer thumps.

LINNEA
Excuse me. Do you know what time the last bus is?

NADIRA
One-ten.

LINNEA
Thanks.

CASIMIR
Ninety-nine percent of the time it's late.

LINNEA
Great.

Nadira pairs two socks, tucks them, sets them on the pile. Casimir rubs his eyes. Linnea turns a page of her textbook. The weather channel shows a swirl of green over the coast.

A phone buzzes on the table. Nadira checks it. Reads. Sets it face down.

CASIMIR
Hospital?

NADIRA
My shift's covered. They just wanted to tell me.

CASIMIR
That's nice of them.

NADIRA
It's the first time.

The intercom crackles again.

INTERCOM (V.O.)
Dryer six is finished. Dryer six has been finished for forty minutes. Dryer six would like you to take your clothes.

Linnea looks up, guilty. Hurries to dryer six. Pulls out a hot tangle of jeans.

LINNEA
Sorry.

CASIMIR
He's watching us.

NADIRA
He's always watching us.

She laughs, quiet. Casimir smiles at his shoes. Linnea folds her jeans into a small, neat square.

Outside, a bus idles at the curb. Nobody gets up.
